WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
On a dark night in Mill Valley, Pennsylvania&nbsp;
in 1968, a young Ramon Morales was in jail. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.000
this particular night he, and a small group&nbsp;
of teenagers, had been besieged by monsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.140
written into life by an angry spirit. One by&nbsp;
one, the group was picked off by creatures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.074
should only exist between the ancient pages of&nbsp;
a long forgotten book. Now… It was Ramon’s turn.

00:00:18.074 --> 00:00:23.580
– [Jangly Man] “Me Tie Doughty Walker”.
– The Jangly Man was coming.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.840
The 2019 film adaption of the popular horror&nbsp;
book series Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.780
was dutiful in recreating some of the&nbsp;
most popular monsters from the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.740
written by Alvin Schwartz and illustrated by&nbsp;
Stephen Gammel. Fan favorites Harold the Scarecrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.700
and The Pale Lady are among the creatures lifted&nbsp;
from the pages to walk and talk on the big screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.300
whose creature designs were revealed ahead&nbsp;
of the film. The Scary Stories fandom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.260
other Super Bowl viewers were stunned at that&nbsp;
accuracy to Gammel’s original infamous artwork.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.800
The Jangly Man, twisting and tearing&nbsp;
its body apart in pursuit of Ramon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.160
was received with awe as well for the&nbsp;
intense visage of its off-center face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.500
and backwards limbs. There’s just one wrinkle…

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.940
The Jangly Man never existed in the original&nbsp;
Scary Stories series. To learn who, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.960
rather WHAT, The Jangly Man is, stick&nbsp;
around to the end of this video.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.151
This video is sponsored by Guardio.

00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:08.319
(metal music)

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.200
Welcome to Horror History. Normally when&nbsp;
analyzing Horror History, I’d begin with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.920
the source material – in this case the book –&nbsp;
and venture forward from there. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.800
however, we’re starting with&nbsp;
the 2019 film adaptation and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.700
much like the Jangly Man himself, going&nbsp;
“backwards” to the original three-book series.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.520
The film adaptation is less of a&nbsp;
direct continuity with book series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.240
and more of a remix of elements from the&nbsp;
stories. A somewhat comparable example would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.500
be the new “40 Year Trilogy” in the Halloween&nbsp;
universe. In Halloween (2018), Kills, and Ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.620
we see elements of all the old Halloween&nbsp;
movies mixed and matched to tell a new story.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.460
In Scary Stories, the newly added character&nbsp;
Sarah Bellows is the author of the literal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.880
scary stories featured in the movie. In the&nbsp;
past, the Bellows were a prominent family in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.300
Mill Valley. Though active in the community&nbsp;
and respected, there was a hidden dark side.

00:01:52.787 --> 00:01:56.880
– [Stella] The Bellows had a secret,&nbsp;
a daughter they never allowed to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.180
the house. The myth is that there&nbsp;
was something really off about her.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.320
To keep her silence, Sarah was imprisoned in&nbsp;
the basement until her death. But legend held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.620
that she would read her stories through the&nbsp;
walls to curious children on the other side.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.640
Fast forward to the 1960s where a group of&nbsp;
socially awkward teens find Sarah’s magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.800
book and, of course, release the cursed contents&nbsp;
upon themselves and the town itself. RIP Tommy.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.420
(Tommy throwing up hay)

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.120
Here’s where things take a&nbsp;
turn. Original Scary Stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.240
to Tell in the Dark book creatures are&nbsp;
definitely present, but The Jangly Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.360
who appears as the final boss in the&nbsp;
film’s finale is not one of them.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.860
– [Stella] Oh my god, what is that?

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.000
– I said the same thing. However, there are&nbsp;
many familiar elements. So in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.680
understand – The Jangly Man – hey, I kind of like&nbsp;
that. We need to pull him apart piece by piece.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.578
(roar)

00:02:39.578 --> 00:02:44.640
(mysterious music)

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.060
Released between the early 80s and 90s,&nbsp;
Alvin Schwartz’s frightening plots and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.540
Stephen Gammel’s macabre illustrations&nbsp;
were inspired by, if not a retelling of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.020
existing urban legends, historical&nbsp;
accounts, or folklore and folktales.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.480
The Jangly Man was inspired&nbsp;
by THREE of these tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.180
all included in the first&nbsp;
entry, Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.000
“Aaron Kelly’s Bones”, “What Do&nbsp;
You Come For?” And of course…

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.080
– [Jangly] Me tie doughty walker

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.560
– The Jangly Man would not be the only&nbsp;
example of multiple stories wrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.940
up into one monster from the book. For example,&nbsp;
one sequence is an adaptation of “The Big Toe”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.520
from the first book, but the monster&nbsp;
with the missing toe looks more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.340
the illustration from “The Haunted&nbsp;
House”, also from the first book.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.580
To stitch together the origins and&nbsp;
dynamic of The Jangly Man, we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.060
to understand what all three of these stories&nbsp;
have in common and where they all originate.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.220
Let’s start with the opening&nbsp;
of “Aaron Kelly’s Bones”:

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.720
Aaron Kelly was dead. They bought him a coffin&nbsp;
and had a funeral and buried him. But that night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:37.440
he got out of his coffin, and he came home. His&nbsp;
family was sitting around when he walked in.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.140
Let’s compare this to the opening&nbsp;
of “What Do You Come For?”

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.980
There was an old woman who lived all by&nbsp;
herself, and she was very lonely. Sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.940
in the kitchen one night she said, “Oh,&nbsp;
I wish I had some company.” No sooner had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.900
she spoken then down the chimney tumbled&nbsp;
two feet from which the flesh had rotted.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.780
Finally, we have the opening&nbsp;
of “Me Tie Dough-ty Walker”:

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.800
There was a haunted house where every night&nbsp;
a bloody head fell down the chimney. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.740
least that’s what people said. So&nbsp;
nobody would stay there overnight.&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.700
Then the rich man offered two hundred&nbsp;
dollars to whoever would do it. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.480
boy said he would try if he could have&nbsp;
his dog with him. So it was all settled.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.420
So far we have all the activity&nbsp;
taking place at night, at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.960
and each involves the idea of company&nbsp;
coming over. As all three stories progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.240
the element of separated limbs is introduced.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.360
In “Aaron Kelly’s Bones”, the titular&nbsp;
character dances himself apart:

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.580
The fiddler fiddled, and dead Aaron danced. Then&nbsp;
Aaron fell apart, collapsed into a pile of bones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.960
– all except his bald headbone that grinned at&nbsp;
the fiddler, cracked its teeth –and kept dancing.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.280
In “What Do You Come For?”

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.120
A body tumbled down, then two arms,&nbsp;
and a man’s head. As the woman watched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.520
the parts came together&nbsp;
into a great, gangling man.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.380
And “Me Tie Dough-ty Walker””

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.920
Soon the boy heard the singing again.&nbsp;
Now it was coming down the chimney:&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.900
♪ Me tie dough-ty walker! ♪
The dog sang right back:&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:56.340
♪ Lynchee kinchy colly molly dingo dingo! ♪
Suddenly a bloody head fell out of the chimney.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.580
Despite the parallels listed above,&nbsp;
there’s no singular entity that each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.640
of these stories seems to be inspired&nbsp;
or derived from. And that’s because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.240
The Jangly Man isn’t a legend or a&nbsp;
myth. This is folklore. And folklore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.540
rarely deals with a literal creature,&nbsp;
but rather explores traditional concepts.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.240
And the concept covered by “Aaron&nbsp;
Kelly’s Bones”, “What Do You Come For”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.668
and “Me Tie Dough-ty Walker” that is eventually&nbsp;
expanded into the 2019’s film... is death.

00:05:16.668 --> 00:05:18.240
– [Ramon] The Jangly Man is coming.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.140
