00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.300
As with most folklore and Scary Stories, there&nbsp;
will never really be an original source of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.920
tale. Even if there once was, by definition:&nbsp;
folklore and folktales were first shared by word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.240
of mouth, not written down. The origins of some&nbsp;
folklore can stretch back past the middle ages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.780
meaning the details, and even the tone, have&nbsp;
changed depending on the culture of the time.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.800
“Harold” out of Scary Stories 3 is a good&nbsp;
example. He’s often referred to as a scarecrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.060
yet, the term “scarecrow” is never used in the&nbsp;
original Scary Stories entry nor, when traced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.720
back to his origins in the alpine regions of&nbsp;
Europe, does the word “scarecrow” ever come up.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.380
The Jangly Man enters Scary Stories to&nbsp;
Tell in the Dark at the climatic ending.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.580
This monster has easily the longest run time&nbsp;
in the film and is introduced by Ramon as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.300
“camp story” that really scared him as a kid.&nbsp;
Our two remaining heroes, Stella and Ramon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.200
find themselves behind bars because Ramon ran&nbsp;
away after being drafted into the Vietnam War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.100
He didn’t want to end up like this older brother,&nbsp;
who was shipped back to his family, in pieces.

00:07:19.027 --> 00:07:26.220
– [Ramon] Last week when I got my&nbsp;
draft notice… I couldn’t. They just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.180
send you there to die.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.680
– [Zac] As the policeman questions them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.980
the power goes out and their attention is&nbsp;
drawn to the dog standing at the fireplace.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.860
(grunting)

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:40.380
This dog seems to live at the police station, and&nbsp;
fills the role of the chanting dog from “Me Tie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.520
Dough-ty Walker”, although this dog doesn't&nbsp;
speak. He just runs away before this happens.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:49.080
(head bounces down chimney)

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.420
The stunned cop tries to shoot his new friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:55.320
but that doesn’t seem to do much. The head is&nbsp;
soon joined by a cascade of limbs and its torso,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.500
much like in the story “What Do You Come&nbsp;
For?”. Like many of the antagonists we’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.160
seen up to this point, The Jangly Man plays&nbsp;
on its victim’s specific fears. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.540
Ramon’s less-than-happy memories of his brother&nbsp;
arriving back home in pieces after the war.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.740
By the way, the name, The Jangly Man, comes&nbsp;
from the campfire story from his youth. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.800
seems to be a slightly altered version of&nbsp;
the description in “What Do You Come For?”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.920
which mentions the body parts coming&nbsp;
together into a great, gangling man.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.140
And that is exactly what happens.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.500
[high pitched grunting]

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:23.797
[growling]

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.880
The newly formed horror breaks the&nbsp;
cop’s neck and throws him into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.360
room where Stella and Ramon are captive. It&nbsp;
crawls over towards them as if it is a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.080
bent over backwards with its head twisted to&nbsp;
lock eyes with them in an upright position.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.740
A little bit of behind the scenes here – The&nbsp;
Jangly Man is played by a contortionist named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.460
Troy James. The previous year, he appeared&nbsp;
on the competition show America’s Got Talent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:46.200
where he performed his weird… Jangly walk I’ll&nbsp;
call it? And freaked out judges and audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.700
members alike. When he does this on stage, he&nbsp;
does so almost as a performative dance routine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.180
further connecting the movements to those&nbsp;
described in the short story “Aaron Kelly’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.270
Bones”. Also, one of the judges is&nbsp;
pretty spot on with his comments.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.880
– I would love to see you star in a&nbsp;
horror film. 

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:04.260
– Anyway, that’s enough reality TV for one decade.&nbsp;
The Jangly Man tries to get into Ramon’s cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.160
by cracking its skull in half to try to squeeze&nbsp;
through the narrow bars, again, bringing reminders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.820
of the text in “Aaron Kelly’s Bones”, which&nbsp;
describes the dancing corpse ‘cracking his teeth’.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.180
Stella is able to obtain the key and free&nbsp;
both of them before the creature passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:18.840
through the steel bars. With his sights solely&nbsp;
set on Ramon, the boy bravely decides to lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.680
the creature away from Stella in the stolen&nbsp;
police car. But The Jangly Man demonstrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.500
its incredible speed by catching up&nbsp;
and mounting the hood of the vehicle.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.760
In a Michael Myers moment, he uses his hand to&nbsp;
smash the passenger side window from above and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.960
Ramon tries everything he can to shake the living&nbsp;
nightmare loose, but to no avail. He resorts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.560
crashing the car into a large truck, pinning&nbsp;
old Jangly between the two motor vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:41.340
where he falls apart piece by piece, our&nbsp;
last reference to “Aaron Kelly’s Bones”.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.880
With no room to escape, Jangly's body&nbsp;
falls back apart, allowing him to slip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:47.640
down between the police car and the&nbsp;
moving truck, then he reassembles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.120
himself once more. It seems like he can break&nbsp;
himself apart and rebuild himself at will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.840
making him pretty much impossible to defeat.&nbsp;
He tracks Ramon back to the Bellows house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:58.200
where he flips his way onto the second floor&nbsp;
landing... like dude, there are stairs.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.920
He breaks down the door of the room Ramon is&nbsp;
hiding in and stalks around on all fours looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.940
for him. After realizing that Ramon is hiding&nbsp;
under a table, he perches himself on the ceiling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:09.420
and waits for his victim to come out, so he&nbsp;
can drop down and attack. He holds the boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:13.740
up by his neck, choking him, but luckily,&nbsp;
for Ramon, The Jangly Man falls apart once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:17.160
more as Stella convinces Sarah Bellows&nbsp;
to release her rage and let go of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.820
stories in another room, and the scattered&nbsp;
pieces of his body seem to just disappear.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:25.800
As far as we can tell, that is the end. But it’s&nbsp;
not the end of this viewing session. For you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.180
Because I’ve got other Scary Stories episodes&nbsp;
lined up in that playlist to the left. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:34.500
love horror, remember to subscribe to CZsWorld&nbsp;
for new horrors every week, ring the deathbell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:40.080
and select all notifications, and I will see&nbsp;
you in the next one. Assuming we both survive.

00:10:40.423 --> 00:10:42.423
(spooky dance music)

